Month = {AUG},
Abstract = {Neighborhood conditions are associated with health outcomes, but whether
   individual health behaviors are independent of or associated with the
   settings are not clear. We analyzed the California Health Interview
   Survey (CHIS) (N = 11,152) data to determine if the perceptions and
   behaviors of similar individuals with an income low enough to be
   eligible for SNAP-Ed services differed based on whether they lived in
   high- or low-income neighborhoods. We found that SNAP-Ed eligible
   individuals living in low-income neighborhoods walked for transportation
   more frequently (3.04 times versus 2.38 times, p = 0.001), drank sugary
   beverages more frequently in the past month (2.93 times versus 1.69
   times, p = 0.000), and had a higher risk of obesity than similar
   low-income individuals living in high-income neighborhoods (0.34 versus
   0.26, p = 0.012).},
